<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Keresztes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1596">1596</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Mezőkeresztes, Battle of Haçova, Haçova Muharebesi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Keresztes was fought on 24–26 October 1596 near the village of Mezőkeresztes in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> between a Habsburg-Transylvanian army and the Ottoman army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-iii">Mehmed III</a>, which had just taken the fortress of Eger. The Christian army of about 55,000 men held fortified trenches and on the second day broke into the Ottoman camp, but its soldiers turned to plunder, and a counterattack by grooms, cooks and tent makers, followed by the Ottoman cavalry, routed it. Both sides lost heavily, so the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> could not exploit their victory; 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">sipahis</a> found absent were stripped of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timar">timars</a>, which contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celali-rebellions">Celali rebellions</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Firearms and long wars reduced their value, and after the <b>Battle of Keresztes</b> in 1596 some 30,000 absentees were stripped of their timars.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
